  "stdout": "# Project A.I.D. (Algorithmic Integrity and Distribution)\n## A Pilot Study for Enhancing SNAP Program Integrity through Advanced Analytics\n\n---\n\n### Assumptions\n\n- The de-identified EBT transactional dataset contains approximately 100,000–500,000 monthly transactions from a representative sample of households across multiple states, spanning a minimum 24-month period, with fields including: transaction amount, timestamp (date and time), retailer ID (NAICS or similar classification), retailer category, and a hashed recipient ID.\n- Ground truth labels for known fraudulent or anomalous accounts are available through existing USDA Food and Nutrition Service (FNS) administrative records, but represent only a fraction (estimated 3–8%) of total anomalous activity, necessitating semi-supervised or anomaly-detection approaches.\n- The State Department of Human Services and USDA FNS oversight committees can be briefed at the 6-month and 12-month milestones and will have access to a secure, read-only dashboard for monitoring model development progress.\n- Computational resources are available via a cloud environment compliant with FISMA Moderate requirements, and all model development occurs within this sanctioned infrastructure.\n- The project team comprises data scientists with expertise in behavioral economics, explainable machine learning, and public-sector data governance, as well as at least one advisor from the USDA National Center for Program Integrity.\n\n---\n\n## Title\n\n**Project A.I.D. (Algorithmic Integrity and Distribution): A Pilot Study for Enhancing SNAP Program Integrity through Advanced Analytics**\n\n*A Novel Behavioral-Signature Framework for Next-Generation EBT Risk Scoring Using Explainable Artificial Intelligence*\n\n---\n\n## Background & Motivation\n\n### 1.1 The Challenge\n\nThe Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) is the largest federal nutrition assistance program in the United States, serving approximately 41 million beneficiaries in FY 2023 with total expenditures exceeding $117 billion. Despite robust administrative controls, program integrity challenges persist. USDA FNS estimates that SNAP payment errors range from 5% to 8% of total expenditures, encompassing both fraudulent activities (e.g., trafficking, unauthorized retail sales) and administrative errors (e.g., eligibility misclassification, overpayments).\n\nCurrent program integrity mechanisms primarily rely on:\n...\n\n5. **Theodorakopoulos, L., Theodoropoulou, A., Tsimakis, A., & Halkiopoulos, C.** (2025). *Big Data-Driven Distributed Machine Learning for Scalable Credit Card Fraud Detection Using PySpark, XGBoost, and CatBoost*. Electronics.\n\n6. **Jin, J. & Zhang, Y.** (2025). *The analysis of fraud detection in financial market under machine learning*. Scientific Reports.\n\n7. **NIST.** (2024). *AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0)*. National Institute of Standards and Technology, U.S. Department of Commerce.\n\n8. **Executive Office of the President.** (2023). *Executive Order on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Artificial Intelligence*. Federal Register, 88 FR 76839.\n\n9. **Thaler, R. H.** (1999). *Mental Accounting Matters*. Journal of Behavioral Decision Making, 12(3), 183–206.\n\n10. **USDA Food and Nutrition Service.** (2024). *SNAP Program Integrity Report*. U.S. Department of Agriculture.\n\n11. **7 CFR Part 272.** *Program Integrity Final Rule*. Code of Federal Regulations, Title 7, Part 272.\n\n12. **Fariha, N., et al.** (2025). *Advanced fraud detection using machine learning models: enhancing financial transaction security*. International Journal of Accounting and Economics Studies.\n\n---\n\n*Document prepared under the Data-Driven Governance initiative of the USDA National Center for Program Integrity, in partnership with the State Department of Human Services. Project A.I.D. is a confidential research document intended for internal review and oversight committee dissemination only.*\n",
